<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bamberg Horseman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">before 1237</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> Horseman is a life-size stone statue of a crowned rider in Bamberg Cathedral, carved before 1237. One of the first large equestrian statues since antiquity, its subject is unknown, though suggestions include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-hungary">Saint Stephen of Hungary</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. It became a symbol of German medieval art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bamberg-horseman.jpg" alt="Bamberg Horseman" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ermell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamberger_Reiter_Dom_Bamberg_P1330479.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The statue, in German Der Bamberger Reiter, is the work of an anonymous medieval sculptor. It probably dates from after 1225 and from before the consecration of the cathedral's new building in 1237. It stands on a console at the north pillar of the St. George choir. Whether this was the place intended for it is not known, but the base appears to be original, and its structure would seem to argue against the statue having been moved.</p><p>The rider wears a crown but carries no weapons, and the statue is one of the first to show a horseshoe. Beneath the horse's front hooves is a carving of the Green Man, one of many in medieval sculpture. Kathleen Basford, in her study of these figures, called this Green Man the &quot;dark counterpart&quot; of the horseman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Because the figure stands in a church and is crowned but unarmed, it is believed to represent a particular king, perhaps one who was a saint. One candidate is Emperor Henry II, who is buried in the cathedral with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-ii">Pope Clement II</a>, though he would probably have been shown with the imperial regalia. Another is his brother-in-law, King Stephen I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, seen as stopping his horse and looking towards Henry's tomb. A third theory favours Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> at the time, who paid for much of the rebuilding of the cathedral.</p><p>Hannes Möhring of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> holds that the rider is the Messiah as described in the Book of Revelation. An inventory of the cathedral's statues in 2014 gave rise to another reading: that a large sculptural screen for the eastern choir was begun and abandoned after only a few statues were finished, two other unusual sculptures in the church fitting the plan. In that reconstruction the horseman was one of the Three Wise Men in a scene of the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, his upturned gaze following the Star of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The horseman is counted as the first monumental equestrian statue since classical antiquity. Stefan George wrote a poem about it. His work influenced Claus Schenk Graf von Stauffenberg, the would-be assassin of Hitler, who was a member of the cavalry unit Bamberger Reiter- und Kavallerieregiment 17, the 17th Cavalry Regiment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bamberg Horseman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Romanesque-Gothic cathedral, with the <b>Bamberg Horseman</b> and the tomb of Pope Clement II, symbolises the see's standing.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-bamberg">Prince-Bishopric of Bamberg</a></li></ul></section>
